window.CONTENT["m08-n10"] = {
 understand: `<p>Most property questions come back to one test. Load a specimen and plot <b>stress</b> (force per area, MPa) against <b>strain</b> (change in length over original length). On the first, straight part the material is <b>elastic</b> and springs back. Beyond it, deformation is <b>plastic</b> (permanent).</p>
<div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>On the curve</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Proportional limit</td><td>End of the straight line</td><td>Stress proportional to strain (Hooke's law)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elastic limit</td><td>Practically the same point</td><td>Highest stress with full recovery</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yield strength</td><td>Stress at a set offset (0.1 or 0.2% permanent strain)</td><td>Used because the proportional limit is hard to locate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ultimate strength</td><td>Highest point</td><td>Maximum stress carried</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elastic modulus</td><td>Slope of the straight line</td><td>Stiffness</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resilience</td><td>Area under the elastic part</td><td>Energy stored and returned</td></tr>
<tr><td>Toughness</td><td>Total area to fracture</td><td>Energy to break</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ductility / malleability</td><td>Plastic strain before failure</td><td>In tension / compression; gold highest</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Proportional limit, elastic limit, yield strength and <b>hardness</b> (resistance to indentation) all measure resistance to <b>permanent</b> deformation; modulus measures stiffness. <b>Brittle</b> materials (ceramics, amalgam, cements) barely deform plastically. Flaws concentrate stress at their tips, so bigger flaws mean lower strength, and tension opens them. Hardness tests: Brinell and Rockwell for metals, Vickers, Knoop (shallow, elongated: enamel and brittle materials), Shore A for rubbers.</p>
<p><b>Time, heat, surfaces.</b> <b>Creep</b>: strain grows under constant load. <b>Stress relaxation</b>: force falls at constant strain. <b>Viscoelastic</b>: response depends on loading rate. <b>Fatigue</b>: fracture under repeated loads below the strength. Thermal <b>conductivity</b> is steady heat flow; <b>diffusivity</b> (conductivity divided by density times specific heat) is how fast a temperature change travels, as in a hot sip. Both are high in metals, low in dentine. <b>Adhesion</b> is attraction between unlike molecules, <b>cohesion</b> between like ones. A low contact angle means good <b>wetting</b>.</p>
<p><b>Working metals.</b> Bending a metal cold (<b>cold work</b>) tangles dislocations: yield strength and hardness rise, ductility falls, and a clasp adjusted repeatedly cracks. A <b>wrought</b> alloy has fibrous grain and a higher proportional limit and hardness than the same alloy cast. Heating it (annealing, soldering nearby) recrystallises the grain and loses the gain.</p>
<p><b>Gold casting alloys</b> run from type <b>I</b> (soft: small inlays) through <b>II</b> (inlays) and <b>III</b> (crowns, short bridges) to <b>IV</b> (extra hard: thin sections, long spans, frameworks). Gold falls; hardness and yield strength rise. Types III and IV <b>heat-harden</b> as gold and copper order into a superlattice. Soften at about 700 °C and quench; harden by soaking at 200-450 °C for 15-30 minutes. Copper adds strength and a red tint, silver whitens, palladium and platinum add strength and melting range (palladium whitens strongly), zinc scavenges oxygen, iridium refines grain.</p>
<p><b>Wax, investment, solder, wire.</b> Inlay wax has the highest thermal expansion of dental materials and stores stress that time and warmth release, so patterns are invested promptly. An investment is a <b>refractory</b> (silica) held by a <b>binder</b>: gypsum for gold alloys, phosphate for high-fusing alloys. <b>Flux</b> removes and prevents oxides so solder wets. Round-wire stiffness rises with diameter to the fourth power and falls with length cubed. Stainless steel is stiffest, then beta-titanium, then superelastic nickel-titanium.</p>
<p><b>Why the exam cares.</b> Items name a property and ask for the test, curve region or clinical failure that matches it.</p>`,
 connect: [
  {to:"m08-n08", why:"Metal-ceramic and casting questions use these terms: modulus resists sag over a span, and investments and solders are the lab half of a cast restoration."},
  {to:"m17-n05", why:"Clasp flexibility, wrought versus cast arms and fracture after repeated adjustment are partial denture applications of modulus, proportional limit and cold work."},
  {to:"m22-n05", why:"Orthodontic force depends on wire alloy, diameter and length, and on stress relaxation in elastomerics."},
  {to:"m08-n05", why:"Creep of gamma-2 amalgam and the brittle, compression-strong behaviour of amalgam are textbook examples of these properties."},
  {to:"m08-n07", why:"Snap removal and pour timing of impressions follow from viscoelasticity and stress relaxation."}
 ],
 apply: {
  vignette:"A 63-year-old's removable partial denture has a cast cobalt-chromium clasp on 3.5. The clasp was tightened chairside four times over two years and has now cracked at its shoulder. Which of the following best explains the fracture?",
  options:["Each adjustment cold-worked the arm, raising its hardness and using up its remaining ductility","The alloy's elastic modulus fell with each adjustment until the arm flexed too far","Saliva caused galvanic corrosion of the arm against an amalgam on 3.5","The arm crept slowly under constant occlusal load until it reached its ultimate strength"],
  answer:"Each adjustment cold-worked the arm, raising its hardness and using up its remaining ductility",
  steps:[
   "Step 1: the ask is the mechanism of a clasp fracture after repeated bending.",
   "Step 2: the facts that matter are repeated permanent bending at room temperature and a low-ductility cast base metal alloy.",
   "Step 3: eliminate the falling modulus, because cold work barely changes modulus; it raises yield strength and hardness.",
   "Step 4: eliminate galvanic corrosion (no other metal is described as the cause) and creep (a clasp is not under a constant load, and creep is a feature of low-melting materials such as amalgam).",
   "Step 5: strain hardening explains it. Each bend adds dislocations, ductility falls, and a cast Co-Cr arm, already of low ductility, cracks at the point of greatest bending."
  ]
 },
 teach: {
  prompt:"Two orthodontic archwires of the same size and length are bent the same small amount. One pushes harder; the other can be bent much further before it stays bent. Reconstruct which properties explain each difference and where they sit on the stress-strain curve.",
  model:"The force for a given deflection is set by the elastic modulus, the slope of the straight part of the curve, so the wire that pushes harder has the higher modulus (stainless steel compared with nickel-titanium). How far a wire can be bent and still spring back is set by its working range: the proportional limit (or yield strength) divided by the modulus. A low-modulus wire with a reasonable elastic limit therefore has a long range. Resilience, the area under the elastic part, is the energy the wire stores and returns. Nickel-titanium also shows superelasticity: a plateau of nearly constant, light force over a wide deflection, which is why it is used for initial alignment."
 },
 cards: [
  ["Stiffness is measured by","Elastic modulus: slope of the elastic part of the stress-strain curve"],
  ["Four measures of resistance to permanent deformation","Proportional limit, elastic limit, yield strength, hardness"],
  ["Resilience vs toughness","Resilience: area under the elastic part (energy returned). Toughness: total area to fracture (energy to break)"],
  ["Why yield strength is quoted instead of proportional limit","The proportional limit is hard to locate; yield is read at a set offset (0.1 or 0.2% permanent strain)"],
  ["Hardness test suited to enamel and brittle materials","Knoop (shallow, elongated diamond indenter)"],
  ["Creep vs stress relaxation","Creep: strain grows under constant load. Relaxation: force falls at constant strain"],
  ["Thermal diffusivity","Conductivity divided by (density x specific heat): how fast a temperature change spreads"],
  ["Effect of cold work on a metal","Higher yield strength and hardness, lower ductility"],
  ["Wrought vs cast form of the same alloy","Wrought: fibrous grain, higher proportional limit and hardness; heating recrystallises it"],
  ["Gold alloy types I to IV trend","Gold falls; hardness and yield strength rise; III and IV can be heat-hardened"],
  ["Purpose of flux in soldering","Dissolves and prevents oxides so the molten solder wets and flows"],
  ["Round wire stiffness vs diameter and length","Proportional to diameter to the 4th power; inversely proportional to length cubed"]
 ],
 questions: [
  ["The stiffness of a dental alloy is best indicated by its:","Elastic modulus",["Ultimate tensile strength","Percentage elongation","Fracture toughness"],"Stiffness is the slope of the elastic part of the stress-strain curve. Ultimate strength is the highest stress, and elongation measures ductility."],
  ["Which property best describes the resistance of a clasp arm to permanent bending?","Yield strength",["Elastic modulus","Toughness","Thermal diffusivity"],"Yield strength, like the proportional and elastic limits, marks the start of permanent deformation. Modulus governs how much the arm flexes elastically, not when it stays bent."],
  ["The energy an orthodontic spring stores when loaded and returns on unloading, without permanent deformation, is its:","Resilience",["Toughness","Ductility","Hardness"],"Resilience is the area under the elastic part of the curve. Toughness is the total area up to fracture, including plastic deformation."],
  ["A long-span metal-ceramic bridge is planned. A base metal alloy is preferred to a high-gold alloy chiefly because its higher elastic modulus:","Reduces flexing of the framework that would crack the porcelain",["Improves the chemical bond between its oxide layer and the porcelain","Lowers the casting shrinkage of the framework","Raises its thermal expansion well above that of the porcelain"],"Porcelain cannot follow a framework that bends, so a stiffer alloy (roughly twice the modulus of gold alloys) protects it over a long span. The modulus has no direct link to bonding or shrinkage."],
  ["Compared with a cast clasp of the same alloy, a wrought wire clasp has a higher proportional limit mainly because of its:","Fibrous grain structure produced by cold working",["Larger, more uniform grains produced by slow cooling","Lower carbon content after drawing","Greater thickness at the shoulder of the clasp arm"],"Drawing the wire elongates the grains and adds dislocations, raising its proportional limit and hardness. Large grains from slow cooling make an alloy weaker, not stronger."],
  ["A technician solders a wrought wire onto a cast framework using a large flame held for a long time. The wire is most likely to:","Lose strength, because heat recrystallises its worked grain structure",["Gain strength, because heat adds further cold work to the wire","Become brittle, because oxides form throughout the inside of the wire","Expand permanently and lose its fit"],"Heating a wrought alloy above its recrystallisation temperature replaces the fibrous worked grains with equiaxed ones, and its higher proportional limit is lost. Heat input near wrought components is kept to a minimum."],
  ["A thin-section cast gold framework for a partial denture should be made from which type of gold casting alloy?","Type IV",["Type I","Type II","Type III"],"Type IV is the extra-hard alloy, with the highest yield strength and hardness, for frameworks and long or thin sections. Type I is soft and used only for small inlays under low stress."],
  ["The hardening heat treatment of a type IV gold alloy strengthens it mainly by:","Forming an ordered gold-copper superlattice",["Dissolving the copper into a disordered solid solution","Recrystallising the grains to a smaller size","Driving off hydrogen absorbed by the palladium"],"A soak at 200-450 °C lets gold and copper atoms order, which blocks dislocations. Heating to about 700 °C and quenching does the reverse: it disorders the lattice and softens the alloy."],
  ["A small amount of which metal has the strongest whitening effect on a gold alloy?","Palladium",["Copper","Zinc","Indium"],"Palladium whitens gold strongly. Copper gives a reddish tint, zinc is an oxygen scavenger, and indium helps form the oxide in metal-ceramic alloys."],
  ["Flux is applied to a joint before gold soldering mainly to:","Remove and prevent oxides so that molten solder wets the joint",["Lower the melting range of the solder below that of the parts","Confine the solder to the joint area and stop it spreading","Increase the strength of the soldered joint by alloying with it"],"Solder only flows on a clean, oxide-free surface, so flux dissolves oxide and shields the joint. Confining flow is the job of antiflux, such as graphite."],
  ["An orthodontist replaces a 0.4 mm round wire with a 0.8 mm wire of the same alloy and length. The stiffness of the new wire is about:","16 times greater",["2 times greater","4 times greater","8 times greater"],"For round wire, stiffness varies with diameter to the fourth power, so doubling the diameter gives 2 to the 4th, or 16. Strength rises with diameter cubed, which is where the tempting 8 comes from."],
  ["A clinician adds a loop to an archwire segment, increasing its effective length. The main effect is:","Lower stiffness, so a lighter force for the same activation",["Higher stiffness, so a heavier force for the same activation","No change in force, because the alloy itself is unchanged","Lower yield strength of the alloy where the loop is bent"],"Stiffness falls with the cube of length, so a longer wire delivers lighter, more constant force. The alloy's own properties stay the same; the geometry is what changes."],
  ["The archwire best suited to initial alignment of crowded teeth, because it gives light force over large deflections, is:","Superelastic nickel-titanium",["Stainless steel","Cobalt-chromium-nickel","Beta-titanium"],"Superelastic nickel-titanium has very low stiffness and a plateau of near-constant force as it changes phase. Stainless steel is stiff and is used for finishing and stable mechanics."],
  ["A patient feels a sharp pain under a new deep amalgam when sipping hot coffee, but not under a composite of the same depth. The property that best explains the difference is the amalgam's higher:","Thermal diffusivity",["Elastic modulus","Coefficient of thermal expansion","Compressive strength"],"A brief hot sip is a transient temperature change, and how quickly it reaches the pulp depends on diffusivity. Metal restorations diffuse heat fast, so a deep one needs remaining dentine or an insulating base."],
  ["A wax pattern for an inlay was left on the bench overnight before investing. The finished casting does not fit. The most likely reason is:","Release of stresses stored in the wax during adaptation",["Absorption of water from the air into the body of the wax","Contraction of the investment around the cold pattern","Chemical reaction between the wax and the die stone"],"Wax has high thermal expansion and keeps residual stress from shaping, which relaxes with time and warmth and changes the pattern's shape. Patterns are therefore invested promptly."]
 ]
};

